            <ArticleTitle>A case of multifocal medulloblastoma in an adult patient.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>45-48</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S1699-8855(16)00020-9</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.patol.2015.12.005</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Only five cases of multifocal medulloblastoma in the adult have been reported to date. We present a case in a male patient in his 50th decade of life who presented with three extra-axial lesions associated with a parenchymatous lesion of the right middle cerebellar peduncle. Sputum sample examination revealed larvae compatible with strongyloides stercoralis, which was our main differential diagnosis. Histological and immunohistochemical studies revealed the existence of a desmoplastic medulloblastoma.</AbstractText>
